# Decided
- Scope: the 47 Purposes of dev/ast/, plus vocabulary/algorithms/
  ddl_predicates.h and field_predicates.h. The hand-over's examples
  (limit.h "each spelling SQLite accepts", cast.h "the SQLite type of a C++
  type") were cut earlier, in da22013e.
- trueqbit's rulings, numbered as put:
  1. match.h: "represent the MATCH condition of a full-text search." ->
     "represent the MATCH operator." https://www.sqlite.org/lang_expr.html#like
     ("The MATCH operator is a special syntax for the match()
     application-defined function"); https://www.sqlite.org/rtree.html#customquery
     (`WHERE id MATCH circle(45.3, 22.9, 5.0)`). A serializer test pins
     R*Tree (5112ca0d); the missing geometry callback API goes to TODO.md
     (46065925).
  2. negated_condition.h kept: "represent the NOT operator applied to a
     condition." trueqbit: NOT flips a condition; an operand that is no
     condition is read as one. https://www.sqlite.org/lang_expr.html#booleanexpr
     converts to a truth value by a NUMERIC cast, and names WHERE, ON/USING,
     HAVING and WHEN as its contexts; on NOT's own conversion the page is
     silent. "Applied to a condition" says how NOT reads its operand.
  3. rowset_deduplicators.h kept. trueqbit: the elements an aggregate reads
     form a rowset logically - SQLite calls the aggregate for each element of
     the set X yields. https://www.sqlite.org/lang_aggfunc.html says
     "duplicate elements are filtered"; "rowset" is sqlite_orm's reading,
     kept as decision 13 keeps a deliberate qualifier.
  4. crud/set.h: "represent the SET clause of an UPDATE." -> "represent the
     SET clause." https://www.sqlite.org/lang_upsert.html (DO UPDATE SET);
     sqlite_orm spells it as on_conflict(...).do_update(set(...)).
  5. operators.h: assign_t is the `=` of SET, which
     https://www.sqlite.org/lang_update.html calls an assignment, no
     expression operator. trueqbit: split out binary_operator; assign_t goes
     to ast/assignment.h ("represent the assignment of a SET clause"),
     binary_operator to ast/binary_operator.h ("give the binary operator
     nodes their shared shape"), in a refactor pull request of its own from
     dev (R2). operators.h's Purpose then holds unchanged. Weighed: moving
     assign_t to crud/set.h (quoted_expression.h would include crud/,
     against ruling 2); its own node struct (its serializer, traversal and
     result type duplicated); keeping it.
  6. crud/default_values.h: "the DEFAULT VALUES modifier." -> "the DEFAULT
     VALUES form of an INSERT." https://www.sqlite.org/lang_insert.html
     ("the third form of an INSERT statement is with DEFAULT VALUES"); REPLACE
     is an alias of INSERT OR REPLACE (https://www.sqlite.org/lang_replace.html).
     The factory's doc-comment follows.
  7. values.h: "the VALUES row list." -> "the VALUES clause."
     https://www.sqlite.org/lang_select.html#values ("The VALUES clause").
     Ruling 33 stands; the Note and dynamic_values_t's doc-comment follow.
  8. crud/into.h: "the INTO clause." -> "the table an INSERT or REPLACE
     writes into." lang_insert and lang_replace name no INTO clause; INTO is
     a keyword of the INSERT syntax.
  9. special_keywords.h: "the keywords SQL evaluates to a value of their
     own" widens past the file (NULL, TRUE, FALSE fit too). Claude's first
     lean narrowed the Purpose to the current date and time, below the
     plural name - withdrawn against campaign.md "Form" (a plural name names
     a kind the file is open to). trueqbit: rename to keyword_literals.h in
     a pull request of its own from dev (R1), Purpose "represent the
     keywords SQL reads as literal values" (https://www.sqlite.org/lang_expr.html,
     literal-value).
  10. "condition" stays in between.h, in.h, exists.h, is_null.h,
     pattern_matching.h, binary_condition.h: the meaning holds; lang_expr
     says "operator", the DSL "condition" (condition_t), and in C++
     "operator" reads as `operator`.
  11. alias.h: "the aliases by which a statement refers to what it reads."
     -> "the aliases a statement gives to what it reads and what it
     returns." as_t and column_alias name result columns
     (https://www.sqlite.org/lang_select.html, `expr AS column-alias`).
- Held against their pages: app_function, between, builtin_function,
  case_expression, cast, collate, compound_operator, cte, excluded, exists,
  from, group_by, in, is_null, join, limit, offset, literal, order_by,
  pattern_matching, result_columns, rowid, select, insert, replace, remove,
  update, upsert_clause ("of an INSERT" keeps it apart from lang_conflict's
  ON CONFLICT), where, window. Naming no SQL construct: operand_tags,
  quoted_expression, column_pointer, table_reference, crud/get,
  crud/set_definitions, ddl_predicates, field_predicates.

